<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">summon-steppe-spirit</ansi> spell

The <ansi fg="command">Summon Steppe Spirit</ansi> spell calls forth a spirit wolf from the
steppe's spiritual plane, binding it to the caster's will. The spirit
fights with the ferocity of a wild predator and persists until
dismissed or destroyed in battle.

The wolf is a moderate thing to keep, well within reach of a summoner
who is not already carrying a heavy bond.

<ansi fg="yellow">Usage: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">cast summon-steppe-spirit</ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow">Details: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Type:        </ansi> Help Single
  <ansi fg="yellow">School:      </ansi> Vital
  <ansi fg="yellow">Base Folds:  </ansi> Brief channel
  <ansi fg="yellow">Cast Cost:   </ansi> Modest, and paid only once
  <ansi fg="yellow">Held Cost:   </ansi> Modest, for as long as you keep it

<ansi fg="white-bold">Notes:</ansi>
  - The spirit wolf fights alongside you as a loyal companion.
  - Spiritual entities ignore terrain hazards that hinder living allies.
  - Summoning it costs conviction once. Keeping it sets conviction
    aside for as long as it stays with you, and that held share,
    not the casting price, is what limits how many you can field.
  - A mightier companion holds more of your will. That is the real
    trade between one companion and another.
  - Skill in manifestation lowers what a companion holds, so
    practice buys you a larger following as well as a stronger one.
  - A summoned spirit is the same wherever you stand. No remains
    are needed and none are consumed.
  - If your conviction is already spoken for, the summoning is
    refused outright rather than leaving you weaker, and costs
    you nothing.

